  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/centrodoras-brachiatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Centrodoras brachiatus is a catfish that grows up to 40 cm long. It was described scientifically in 1872.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/centrodoras-brachiatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Although the species has a large distribution area, practically nothing is known about its wild life.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/centrodoras-brachiatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>The large tooth cushion in the lower jaw indicates that the animals may graze on growth.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/centrodoras-brachiatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>The specimens often \"hold their breath\": after strong breathing movements they stop breathing again and again for a longer time (at least nothing can be seen of it). This is a behaviour that has not yet been observed in other fish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/centrodoras-brachiatus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Typical is the form of the lateral bone row, whose shields are very big directly under the dorsal fin and then quickly become much smaller towards the tail. The species is only extremely rarely imported.</p>"
    }
  ],
